From be9a6ac92c4ebd6bb00ac184ec3881f72cb5b176 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Andreas=20F=C3=A4rber?= <afaerber@suse.de>
Date: Sun, 22 Nov 2026 12:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 03/40] irqchip: Add Realtek RTD1195/RTD1295 IRQ mux driver
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The ISO and MISC register blocks of the Realtek RTD1195,
RTD1295/RTD1296, RTD1395 and RTD1619 SoCs each combine the interrupts of
their low-speed peripherals into a single GIC interrupt. A source's
status bit (ISR) and its SCPU enable bit (SCPU_INT_EN) do not always
have the same index, so a per-SoC table maps one to the other.

Without this driver none of these peripherals can use interrupts: the
UARTs have to be polled, and GPIO and I2C interrupts cannot be used at
all.

The driver is based in part on QNAP's rtk_irq_mux.c and Synology's
irq-rtk.c. The RTD1295 tables match the irq_map_tab of the Realtek 4.9
BSP (irq-rtd129x.h).

Ported from Andreas Färber's work-in-progress RTD1295 tree to Linux
6.18. Changes in the port:
- Use irq_domain_create_linear() and generic_handle_domain_irq().
- Only dispatch ISR bits whose enable bit is set; ISR also latches
  sources that are not routed to the SCPU.
- Use a const irq_chip with irq_print_chip instead of a per-mux copy,
  mark the interrupts as level type and skip set_wake.
- Drop the NMI table entries, and refuse to map hwirqs beyond the
  32-bit status register.
- Fix the clear-all write at init (W=1 -Woverflow) and the error path,
  which leaked the ioremap and the parent IRQ mapping.
